File Index

/useFloatingPanel.ts
Purpose: Drag, resize and clamp for a window that must not block the page behind it. Pointer events (mouse, pen, finger); drag starts only from non-control targets of the header; geometry is saved on release, restored clamped, and re-clamped on a browser resize. Resizing is capped at the viewport edge WITHOUT moving the panel (clampGeometry alone would shove it sideways to make room). It reads the bare `localStorage` global, not window.localStorage: both are the same in a browser, but jest-jsdom only lets a test block the bare one. Tests: useFloatingPanel.test.tsx (jsdom has no PointerEvent: they dispatch MouseEvents named pointermove).

/useAutoLeveling.ts (autopilot signals)
Purpose: It calls useAutopilotSignals(runState) so other features (the Copilot gate) learn Autopilot's status and error without importing the engine. The file uses import.meta, which the client jest config cannot compile, so it has no hook test of its own; the emitted events are proven in useAutopilotSignals.test.tsx and autopilot-signals.test.ts.

/useBeforeUnloadGuard.ts
Purpose: Attaches ONE beforeunload listener for the component's whole life; `active` is copied to a ref in a layout effect (a render-time write trips react-hooks/refs) and read when the event fires, so there is never a detach window when `active` flips. Browsers ignore custom text and only prompt after user interaction (sticky activation), which a script cannot override. Flipping `active` on a mounted component never re-attaches the listener, and the listener is removed on unmount. Test: useBeforeUnloadGuard.test.tsx.

/usePlugins.ts
Purpose: The installed-plugin store (one global localStorage key). ALL writers (the hook, writeInstalledPlugins for cloud load and file import, plugin self-patches) end in persistRecords, which prunes each record's userConfig to the values that differ from the plugin's defaults; a record for an unregistered plugin is stored as given. migratePluginConfigOverrides prunes already-frozen stores once at module load (marker key, written only after the prune is written; storage failures leave the data alone and retry next start). Design and gotchas: docs/game/plugin-config.md.

/useAccountModal.ts
Purpose: Phase D — Account modal's state/actions: login (awaits gameSso.startLogin's popup and maps its LoginOutcome to a status line, with its own `loginPending` flag kept separate from `busy` so the cloud buttons aren't affected; a subscribeToToken subscription flips isLoggedIn the moment the popup writes, since nothing in this window re-runs on its own), logout (local token clear only), and save/load-to-cloud for the CURRENT connection's scripts + plugins via cloudSync.ts. Save reads through UserScriptRuntime's public loadScriptsFromStorage/getStorageKey (no private-API reach); Load writes localStorage directly for both scripts and the global plugins key, then does a full page reload rather than trying to live-patch every derived runtime/plugin-host state — deliberately simpler and safer for a destructive, user-confirmed action. Phase E folded librarySync.ts's saveLibraryToCloud/loadLibraryFromCloud into the same Promise.all pair — unlike scripts/plugins, Load MERGES library content (never deletes local writings), so the confirm-dialog copy was reworded to say so accurately. Autoleveling redesign step 12 added a fifth leg the same way: autoleveling-user-data.ts's exportAll()/importRecords() round-trip through cloudSync.saveAutolevelUserData/loadAutolevelUserData — Save reads exportAll() before the Promise.all (its own await, not parallelized, since the others don't need it first), Load calls importRecords() on the fetched array right after the error checks; this is a MERGE like library content (importRecords is last-writer-wins by updatedAt), not a replace. Cloud Save/Load safety (2026-10): Load is fetch-first and confirms through features/auth/loadImpact.ts before writing; the library merge runs after the confirm because it writes IndexedDB itself; a load that changed something reloads after RELOAD_DELAY_MS so the result stays readable; the server omits count when 0, so counts are read with ?? 0. Tests: useAccountModal.test.tsx. Change-together: useAccountModal.ts, features/auth/loadImpact.ts, features/userScripts/globalScriptsStore.ts (normalizeGlobalBuckets / replaceGlobalScriptBuckets) and components/AccountModal.tsx's help text — a change to what Load replaces must be reflected in all four, or the confirm will promise something the apply step does not do.
Tests: useAccountModal.test.tsx mocks cloudSync and the autoleveling store and asserts the ORDER import(cloud copy) -> export -> upload for Save, the 1000-record stop before any upload, and that a non-list cloud answer counts as empty for every collection. The Save merge only adds and updates records: deleting one on this device does not remove it from the cloud copy.
Context (2026-11-13): Save also reads the CLOUD auto-leveling collection and merges it into this device (importRecords, newest updatedAt wins) BEFORE exportAll, because the server PUT replaces the whole collection and a device that never loaded would wipe another device's target edits (CLOUDSYNC-02); a failed/unauthenticated look-ahead stops the save, and a union over AUTOLEVEL_MAX_RECORDS (1000) stops it before ANY upload. The merge never deletes. Load names how many records were target edits.

/useAutoLeveling.ts
Purpose: Owns the persisted `AutoLevelConfig` (per-connection localStorage) and the `AutoLevelingEngine`'s lifetime — (re)creates/binds/unbinds the engine on `connectionId` change, tracks `socketReady` off the game socket's open/close events (gates Start), and exposes `runState`/`xpProgress` plus start/stop/pause/resume/resetToDefaults/moveNext/movePrev/rescanRoom, all also reachable via `shatteredarchive:autoleveling-*` window events for external UI (compass etc). The hook owns config persistence + engine lifetime; the engine (autoleveling-engine.ts) owns all runtime behavior. Wires two `EngineDeps` added 2026-09 for live-playtest fixes: `getLearnedCooldown`/`onAbilityCooldownLearned` (bumpLearnedCooldown) round-trip the per-ability auto-tuned cooldown store in autoleveling-user-data.ts; `sendThroughCommandProcessor` routes rest commands through `RuntimeSingleton.Runtime.executeAlias` (falling back to the raw `shatteredarchive:send-command` event if no Runtime is bound) — the same alias-expansion path `useGameCommand.ts` uses for manually-typed terminal input, so a rest sequence built as the player's own alias/macro actually expands instead of being sent to the MUD verbatim.

/useAuthCallback.ts
Purpose: Phase D — on mount, reads window.location.hash for the game-sso hand-off's result (features/auth/authFragment.ts's parseAuthFragment), stores a successful token via authTokenStore, then strips the fragment via history.replaceState so it never lingers in browser history. No router exists in this app, so this runs as a plain effect from MainContainer rather than a dedicated callback route. NOT the live path since the popup rewrite (2026-07-31) — src/auth-callback.ts handles the hand-off now. Kept as the safety net for a hand-off whose returnUrl still points at the app itself: an attempt already in flight across the deploy, or an old bookmarked link. Do not "clean up" without checking for those.

/useCompactLayoutSizing.ts
Purpose: HUD compact layout support — mirrors useMainContainer.ts's useLayoutSizing (same clamp/localStorage-backed useState/persist-effect/mousedown-drag shape) but under its own localStorage keys (shatteredArchive.compactLayout.rightPaneWidth / .chatPaneHeight) so compact-layout sizing doesn't collide with classic layout's. Exposes --right-pane-width and --sa-chat-pane-height CSS vars plus handleVerticalResizeMouseDown/handleChatResizeMouseDown drag handlers. Consumed by Task 11's CompactLayoutShell.

/useCharacterIdentity.ts
Purpose: HUD compact layout support — provides the current character name via window.__SA_IDENTITY__ snapshot and shatteredarchive:identity-updated event subscription. Generates unique listener keys per instance via useId() to support multiple simultaneous consumers (CompactLayoutShell and CompactRoomRow) without event-listener stomping; each hook instance independently seeds from snapshot on mount and updates on event dispatch.

/useLevelProgress.ts
Purpose: HUD compact layout support — React binding (useSyncExternalStore) over features/charData/levelProgressStore for the EXP gauge in CompactVitalsRow; all state lives in the store so it survives layout switches.

/useOpponentStatus.ts
Purpose: HUD compact layout support — extracted verbatim from RightSidebar.tsx's StatusBlock (2026-09-14): tracks the enemy/opponent combat bar via 'event:fighting:opponent' (200ms staleness ticker that runs ONLY while an enemy is active (an always-on one re-rendered every enemy bar 5x/s while idle; tests count renders), ENEMY_STALE_MS=5000, damage-chunk pulse cleared after 4500ms). Consumed by classic StatusBlock and (later) CompactVitalsRow, so — matching useCharacterIdentity's fix for the same class of bug — its ListenEvent key is suffixed with useId() per instance rather than the single static key StatusBlock used inline, so two simultaneous consumers don't evict each other's listener via the shared event registry. 2026-09-20 (live HUD theme switching): seeds `enemyUi` from features/combat/opponentStatusStore.ts's cache on mount and writes every update back to it, so remounting this hook (a live theme swap) doesn't reset to "no enemy" — isEnemyActive stays correctly derived from lastSeenTs vs. the live clock either way, so a stale cache still reads as inactive. 2026-10-31: every consumer mounts its OWN instance (classic StatusBlock, CompactVitalsRow, CompactWidgetSlot), so anything the hook does on a timer is multiplied per instance — the staleness clock is deliberately started only while lastSeenTs is inside ENEMY_STALE_MS and stops itself when stale; keep it that way (an always-on setNow re-rendered each bar 5x/s while idle). useOpponentStatus.test.ts counts renders to guard this.

/useAffectsBlock.ts
Purpose: The server holds an affect at d = 0 for its last tick, then sends remove_affect (cut eyes 2026-11-01: d 2 -> 0, wear-off line + remove_affect ~30 s later). So an affect is NEVER dropped for its duration: only game:affect-removed (or a trueup snapshot that omits it) removes one, and the local tick floors d at 0. AffectsBlock.tsx renders d <= 0 red and useSanctuaryActive likewise ignores d, so a duration filter here makes the list disagree with the glow. Gotcha: events arrive through DispatchEvent, which spreads the payload into a plain object, so the handlers' Array.isArray(payload) branch and the sort's final `t` tie-break (entries tying on n/lc/m are already collapsed by the dedupe) are unreachable; tests cannot cover them. Tests: useAffectsBlock.test.ts.

/useAutopilotSignals.ts
Purpose: The one place Autopilot's run state is turned into the events other features listen to (autopilot-status on every change, autopilot-need-help once when it becomes error). It is its own hook because useAutoLeveling.ts uses import.meta and cannot be compiled by the client jest config, so it has no hook test; the logic is in features/autoleveling/autopilot-signals.ts. Tests: useAutopilotSignals.test.tsx.

/useGameCommand.ts (user-typing)
Purpose: Emits shatteredarchive:user-typing {at} whenever the input box holds text and changes: the Copilot command gate stays quiet for 2 s after it (E2). It fires on the box, not on Enter, because Enter clears the box and the typing happened before. Tests: useGameCommand.userTyping.test.tsx.

/useWindowLayer.ts
Purpose: The ONE stack of open modals and floating windows. Its order is both what is drawn on top (z-index 5000 + place, so numbers never grow with the number of drags) and what the Escape key closes (one window per press, the top one). A single document keydown listener exists only while a window is open. A key something inside already used (preventDefault or stopPropagation, as an open dropdown does) never reaches a window, so an inner control that handles Escape must stop it. Every modal and window calls it BEFORE its early return null; a new modal must too, or it has no Escape and no stacking.

/useResizableModal.ts
Purpose: The corner-grip resize for modals that are not floating panels (useFloatingPanel keeps its own). A modal never resized is left exactly as its style sheet draws it; the first resize stores a size (clamped to the screen and a minimum, saved on release). A centered overlay modal grows on both sides, so the pointer moves twice as far as the corner (centered: true). The root becomes position: relative when it is not positioned, so the grip can sit in its corner. UserScriptSandboxModal has its own older resize and does not use this. Gotcha: DESTRUCTURE the result (`const { attach, style, gripProps } = useResizableModal(...)`), do not read `resize.style`: the React Compiler lint rule react-hooks/refs reports every property read on the returned object as a ref read during render. `attach` is a callback ref (it positions the root and keeps the element), which is why the result holds no ref object.
